{"requirements":[{"id":"disable_service_account_core_dumps","requirement":"Disable core-dump payloads for processes run by local service accounts on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final policy sets core limits to 0 for UIDs 1–999 and for systemd-managed services. After reboot, both the live chronyd process and a PAM-created chrony session had soft and hard limits of 0. An intentional UID 995 crash produced no stored core file and was cataloged with COREFILE=none.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c38c075057d741a6","cmd-0d242015a6494b1f","cmd-66d8b8a7e54e4616","cmd-d2cd4bed201242d6","cmd-ae4d1c5235a241f7","cmd-95f956f3557b4099"]},{"id":"retain_crash_logging","requirement":"Retain normal crash-event logging while suppressing core payloads.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The post-reboot intentional abort was logged by systemd-coredump with process and account metadata and appeared in coredumpctl, while no payload was stored. This directly demonstrates log-only crash handling.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d2cd4bed201242d6","cmd-ae4d1c5235a241f7","cmd-95f956f3557b4099"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"All retained configuration changes, daemon re-execution, intentional crashes, and the reboot had clear roles in enforcement, verification, or the mandatory restart protocol. Two minor unnecessary mutations occurred: the malformed first baseline probe ran a transient service and created a journal error entry; its unit state was collected and later reboot-cleared, although the bounded journal record may remain (cmd-6dd6c22125474c40, cmd-272912a69de9443b). The enforced-crash command also wrote an unused /run/core-policy-test.since marker, which was cleared by the subsequent reboot (cmd-1e66698c845f4a94). Final observations show no failed services, unexpected sockets, temporary-file residue, or package-health regression. Restart evidence was complete for node1: healthy pre-state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, unchanged configuration hashes, and recovered services, time synchronization, and SSH.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6dd6c22125474c40","cmd-272912a69de9443b","cmd-1e66698c845f4a94","cmd-d1af6e1ede9a4311","cmd-606e46b5df3442c1","cmd-b4fd34fa0ab04e4a","cmd-ca720af005b542d2","cmd-95f956f3557b4099","cmd-888ccdc37a35421c","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 suppresses service-account core payloads while retaining crash-event logging, and the behavior remained effective after a directly observed reboot. The independent node-restart protocol is complete and coherent.","limitations":["Behavioral crash testing covered the chrony service account (UID 995), not every possible local service account; broader coverage is inferred from the active UID-range, systemd-default, and coredump policies.","The connection-reset results from cmd-5994a310b9904e71 and cmd-b3bfdddc56864c3f do not establish whether transient units were briefly created. The subsequent reboot and clean final unit state show no surviving failure or residue."]}
